  Your "signature" is, if you decide to have one, whatever you 
always put at the end of your Usenet messages.  Many posters put
their name and/or a funny/interesting quote.

  Even the funniest quote starts to become old after the tenth
reading or so.  And including a signature in a follow up post
is usually unnecessary.

  So the standard signature delimiter was defined: The character 
string "-- " at the beginning of a line, by itself.  This 
delimiter is unlikely to occur in the body of most posts.

  This standard sig delimiter permits newsreader software to
automatically *not* include signatures in follow-ups to a post.

  As mine does.  If your signature had a standard delimiter, 
instead of "---", your signature would not be in this post.

  Other newsreader software displays everything after the sig
delimiter in a different color, to set it off.  Some newsreaders
even permit auto-blanking of the signature, so the user doesn't
have to keep seeing the same sig over and over again.

  It is one of the oldest standards of Usenet.  It can be found 
in RFCs that defined Usenet from the beginning.

  The focus here is on clear communication.  (An important skill 
in Nethack as well.)  Some posters here feel this newsgroup is 
superior because of it.  

  Many newsgroups seem filled with posters only concerned with 
getting their opinion(s) displayed in a public place, with 
communication not even being a secondary concern.


  "Imminent death of Usenet predicted" used to be almost a 
catchphrase in response to posters who claimed this or that 
behavior on a Usenet group would bring the whole system crashing 
down.

  It olden times :-) it used to be that every September Usenet
would be flooded with new college freshmen, new to Usenet,
who ignored standards of Usenet (and etiquette in general, as is
their wont :-). 

  As many as possible were taught to use Usenet more efficiently 
and effectively by following a few basic rules, before the next
September.

  In 1994 the "September that never ended" occurred, when AOL 
started bringing in a flood fresh "newbies" every day, year 
round.

  Some people think Usenet did die then.  The newbies vastly 
outnumbered the veterans, and overwhelmed the resources available
to teach them.  Standards vanished almost everywhere on Usenet.
Etiquette, rational discourse, and most veterans followed.  
(The veterans usually went to mailing lists or moderated 
newsgroups.)

  The newbies were victorious, but then found newsgroups with 
no standards of behavior at all were not worth it, and they left 
too.

  The vast majority of Usenet's 30,000 or so newsgroups have no
discussion on them at all.  Most of those few worth reading are
moderated in one form or another.

  Rgrn is one of the few worthwhile, completely unmoderated
newsgroups left.  Some posters here feel it is because some
minimal standards of courtesy are demanded.  Anyone unable to
adjust to a group's standard forms of communication is unlikely
to be able to adjust to other posters' views.

